1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,724
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 17.

2
00:00:05,344 --> 00:00:09,614
Why update the term with term times equals x over k, instead of

3
00:00:09,614 --> 00:00:13,947
computing pow of x and k over an int factorial on every pass?

4
00:00:15,647 --> 00:00:17,759
Look at how fast factorials grow.

5
00:00:17,759 --> 00:00:22,671
Twelve factorial is four hundred seventy nine million, which still fits in an int.

6
00:00:23,291 --> 00:00:26,476
But thirteen factorial is over six billion, bigger

7
00:00:26,476 --> 00:00:30,073
than the largest int, about two point one billion.

8
00:00:30,073 --> 00:00:32,609
So an int factorial overflows from k equals

9
00:00:32,609 --> 00:00:36,266
thirteen, and this problem needs k up to seventeen.

10
00:00:36,886 --> 00:00:40,556
The incremental update never builds those huge numbers.

11
00:00:40,556 --> 00:00:43,812
Each pass is one multiplication of small values.

12
00:00:45,413 --> 00:00:48,497
The theory: an int is four bytes, so its largest

13
00:00:48,497 --> 00:00:51,415
value is two to the thirty one, minus one.

14
00:00:51,415 --> 00:00:55,875
Overflow does not raise an error: the value silently wraps to garbage.

15
00:00:57,475 --> 00:01:02,142
So the reason is that an int factorial overflows once k reaches thirteen.

16
00:01:02,962 --> 00:01:07,810
And watch the absolute value in the test: for doubles, use fabs, not abs.

17
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:12,980
Exam tip: remember that the int limit is about two point one billion,

18
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:16,795
and that thirteen factorial is the first factorial past it.
